dialogue_history.argument:
A key weak-negation assumption in the target is: **“safety-focused AI doesn’t require the kind of broad individual data collection and profiling/inference pipelines that drive the strongest privacy and discrimination harms.”**

That assumption is often false in practice because “workplace/industrial safety and assistive safety monitoring” frequently depends on data that is *about identifiable people*, not just hazards—e.g.,:
- **Video/image monitoring** to detect unsafe behavior or PPE compliance;
- **Audio capture** (including conversations) for incident detection;
- **Wearables / biometrics** (heart rate, fatigue, gait) to predict impairment;
- **Location/RFID tracking** to determine who is where and when;
- **Interaction/performance logs** used to infer training needs or “risk scores” per worker.

Even if the *intended* objective is safety, these data streams create the same privacy pathway the target tries to bracket off: they enable **re-identification, membership inference, and behavior/performance profiling** about individuals and can be repurposed for discipline or monitoring productivity.

Similarly, the target assumes safety systems don’t carry the discrimination pipeline. But sensor performance and training data can vary across groups (e.g., skin tone/camera detection differences; different mobility/assistive-device users; different workwear/physical characteristics), producing **disparate false positives/false negatives**—which in a workplace context can translate into unequal reprimands or different deployment of safety interventions.

So the “limited-scope deployments” premise doesn’t reliably exclude the very harms (privacy-by-profiling and discrimination) the target claims the safety use-case avoids. That undercuts the target’s attempt to isolate safety AI as a default net-good exception.
